From 4ec1596324768545b34e6ca9620fa0b394cc9d33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 01:19:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] guilib, application: the windows that open over a video,
 loaded ahead while the menus are idle

Issue #3 (cinema-ONE, RX 7600): with the Settings window open over a native Dolby Vision video all
frames kept up, but for one gap of 83 ms as that window opened, one refresh missed on its first
frame. The Settings window is of the KEEP_IN_MEMORY kind (CGUIWindowSettings): it loads its skin
file and makes its controls the first time it opens in a session, and allocates its textures each
time it opens (CGUIWindow::OnMessage(GUI_MSG_WINDOW_INIT), AllocResources()). Opened over a video,
that is done on the thread that also presents the frames of the video. So are the video OSD, its
settings and the player information, which are kept the same way.

Kodi already loads some windows ahead: those of the LOAD_ON_GUI_INIT kind, when the skin loads
(CGUIWindowManager::LoadNotOnDemandWindows(): CGUIWindow::Initialize(), which loads the skin file
and allocates the resources). A skin cannot ask for that: the load type is set by each window's
class, and a skin's own window is made LOAD_ON_GUI_INIT only where it has a visible condition
(CApplicationSkinHandling). Making these windows LOAD_ON_GUI_INIT would load them all at start, in
the skin's load, and keep their textures for the whole session.

Now they are loaded ahead the way Kodi loads those windows, Initialize(), on the application thread,
one a pass of the loop, only while the menus are idle (CWindowPreload::Idle(): the windows are there,
nothing plays, no modal dialog is open, the screen is awake, and no input came for 2 s;
CApplication::Process() asks it while any is left). The list, in the order they are most often
opened over a video: the video OSD (VideoOSD.xml), the player information
(DialogPlayerProcessInfo.xml, with the pages of Hades), the Settings window (Settings.xml), the
video, audio and subtitle settings of the player (DialogSettings.xml) and the information in full
screen (DialogFullScreenInfo.xml). A window is left alone where it is not of the KEEP_IN_MEMORY kind
(Kodi loads it every time, or at start already), where it is loaded or open already, or where the
skin has no file for it. The list starts again when the skin loads (CGUIWindowManager::Initialize()).
Left out: the categories of the settings (a window each, of the same skin file: loading them all
would keep a copy for each), the player information of Hades of its own (only for a skin without its
pages, and Estuary has them), and what opens seldom over a video.

Then the first open of such a window is as its second has always been: the skin file is not read
again, unless conditions of its includes changed since (CGUIWindow::NeedLoad(), as Kodi checks it at
every open), its OnWindowLoaded() ran already (read for the two that have one here: the player
information looks for the properties of Hades in its own file, which does not change; the dialogs of
settings make their controls on each open, OnInitWindow()), and the textures that the load allocated
are taken as they are (CGUITexture::AllocResources() keeps a texture it has). Closing it frees them,
as before (GUI_MSG_WINDOW_DEINIT, dynamic resource allocation). The cost: those windows' textures are
held from the idle moment until each is first opened (or for the session, for one that never is),
and the time of each load falls on an idle pass of the menus. kodi.log says it once, without
debugging, with the time of each (figures made up):

  GUI: 7 windows that open over a video were loaded ahead while the menus were idle, one a pass:
  VideoOSD.xml 9.8 ms, DialogPlayerProcessInfo.xml 31.2 ms, Settings.xml 12.4 ms, ...; 71.0 ms in
  all, the longest 31.2 ms

and each load writes Kodi's own "Loading skin file: ..., load type: KEEP_IN_MEMORY".

Not run in Kodi (its interface does not start in the test machine): the times and the memory are
not measured. Host test (windowpreload_test, 15 checks): the idle rule in each of its conditions,
one window a pass in order and only on idle passes, the line once after the last with each window's
time, the sum, the longest and what became of the others, and the list again after a new skin.
